.Dd April 19, 1999
.Dt KPASSWDD 8
.Os HEIMDAL
.Sh NAME
.Nm kpasswdd
.Nd Kerberos 5 password changing server
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Bk -words
.Op Fl Fl addresses= Ns Ar address
.Op Fl Fl check-library= Ns Ar library
.Op Fl Fl check-function= Ns Ar function
.Oo Fl k Ar kspec \*(Ba Xo
.Fl Fl keytab= Ns Ar kspec
.Xc
.Oc
.Oo Fl r Ar realm \*(Ba Xo
.Fl Fl realm= Ns Ar realm
.Xc
.Oc
.Oo Fl p Ar string \*(Ba Xo
.Fl Fl port= Ns Ar string
.Xc
.Oc
.Op Fl Fl detach
.Op Fl Fl version
.Op Fl Fl help
.Ek
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
serves request for password changes. It listens on UDP port 464
(service kpasswd) and processes requests when they arrive. It changes
the database directly and should thus only run on the master KDC.
.Pp
Supported options:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl Fl addresses= Ns Ar address
For each till the argument is given, add the address to what kpasswdd
should listen too.
.It Fl Fl check-library= Ns Ar library
If your system has support for dynamic loading of shared libraries,
you can use an external function to check password quality. This
option specifies which library to load.
.It Fl Fl check-function= Ns Ar function
This is the function to call in the loaded library. The function
should look like this:
.Pp
.Ft const char *
.Fn passwd_check "krb5_context context" "krb5_principal principal" "krb5_data *password"
.Pp
.Fa context
is an initialized context;
.Fa principal
is the one who tries to change passwords, and
.Fa password
is the new password. Note that the password (in
.Fa password->data )
is not zero terminated.
.It Fl k Ar kspec , Fl Fl keytab= Ns Ar kspec
Keytab to get authentication key from.
.It Fl r Ar realm , Fl Fl realm= Ns Ar realm
Default realm.
.It Fl p Ar string , Fl Fl port= Ns Ar string
Port to listen on (default service kpasswd - 464).
.It Fl Fl detach
Detach from the terminal and run as a daemon; see
.Sx SERVICE MANAGER INTEGRATION .
.El
.Sh SERVICE MANAGER INTEGRATION
.Nm
can be run by
.Xr systemd 1
or a compatible service manager in either of the two ways described
below, and either way can be combined with socket activation.
.Pp
The example unit files
.Pa kpasswdd.service.example
and
.Pa kpasswdd.socket.example
in the
.Pa kpasswd/
directory of the Heimdal source distribution can be used as a
starting point.
.Ss Type=forking
When started with
.Fl Fl detach ,
the process started by the service manager does not exit until the
daemon process that it starts is ready to serve requests, so the
service manager's notion of when
.Nm
is ready is accurate.
The daemon writes its process ID to
.Pa /var/run/kpasswdd.pid ,
or to
.Pa kpasswdd.pid
in the directory named by the
.Ev HEIM_PIDFILE_DIR
environment variable.
Use this with
.Li Type=forking
services, and set
.Li PIDFile=
accordingly (e.g.,
.Li PIDFile=/run/kpasswdd.pid
where
.Pa /var/run
is a symbolic link to
.Pa /run ) .
.Ss Type=notify
When the
.Ev NOTIFY_SOCKET
environment variable is set,
.Nm
sends
.Xr sd_notify 3
messages to the service manager:
.Li READY=1
once it is ready to serve requests,
.Li STATUS=
messages showing how many password change requests have been processed, and
.Li STOPPING=1
when it begins shutting down.
Use this with
.Li Type=notify
services, with or without
.Fl Fl detach .
With
.Fl Fl detach ,
the process started by the service manager waits until the daemon
process is ready, then sends
.Li READY=1
and a
.Li MAINPID=
message naming the daemon process, and exits, so the service manager
tracks the daemon process from then on.
.Pp
.Li Type=forking
services that set
.Li NotifyAccess=
also receive these messages (e.g., to show the
.Li STATUS= ) ;
use
.Li NotifyAccess=exec
or
.Li NotifyAccess=all ,
as with
.Li NotifyAccess=main
systemd logs that it ignored the startup notification.
.Pp
.Nm
sends notifications only at startup, after processing a request, and
at shutdown, so they never cause additional wakeups.
In particular,
.Nm
does not send
.Li WATCHDOG=1
messages, so do not set
.Li WatchdogSec=
for it.
.Ss Socket activation
.Nm
supports systemd-style socket activation (see
.Xr sd_listen_fds 3 ) .
When started with sockets passed in this way it uses them instead of
creating its own, and the
.Fl Fl port
and
.Fl Fl addresses
options are ignored.
.Pp
If any of the passed sockets is named
.Dq kpasswdd
(e.g., with
.Li FileDescriptorName=kpasswdd
in the socket unit) then only those sockets are used; otherwise all
of the passed sockets are used.
Only IPv4 and IPv6 UDP sockets are used.
Any other passed sockets (e.g., of another type or address family)
are closed with a warning, sockets not selected by name are closed
silently, and
.Nm
exits with an error if none of the passed sockets are usable.
.Pp
Replies must come from the address the request was sent to, so on
hosts with more than one address configure one
.Li ListenDatagram=
socket per address rather than a single wildcard socket.
.Pp
Socket activation works with both
.Li Type=forking
and
.Li Type=notify ;
with
.Fl Fl detach
the passed sockets are handed to the detached daemon process.
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
If an error occurs, the error message is returned to the user and/or
logged to syslog.
.Sh BUGS
The default password quality checks are too basic.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr kpasswd 1 ,
.Xr kdc 8
